---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Job Radar — Sebastian Gaggero">
  <main>
    <article class="case-study" aria-labelledby="case-study-title">
      <header class="container-reading introduction">
        <a class="back-link" href="/#work">
          ← Back to work
        </a>
        <h1 id="case-study-title">Job Radar</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          External job offers need a consistent structure before they can be compared, filtered, and
          ranked.
        </p>
        <p>
          Job Radar imports offers from Remotive and Python.org, stores normalized records in
          PostgreSQL, and exposes them through a FastAPI REST API and a simple web interface. Its
          scoring profile targets junior remote Python/backend roles—the kind of work I’m looking
          for.
        </p>
        <p>
          The engineering work centers on interpreting source data, deciding what counts as a
          duplicate, and making ranking behavior predictable.
        </p>
        <a
          class="project-link"
          href="https://job-radar-bonw.onrender.com/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Open live project →
        </a>
      </header>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="normalization-title">
        <p class="section-marker">01 / SOURCES</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="normalization-title">Two sources, one record shape</h2>
          <p>
            Remotive supplies JSON with job descriptions. Python.org provides an HTML listing page.
            Its importer extracts the available fields and categories without following individual
            job pages.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both importers produce the same record shape. Source-specific company and location
            fields become shared fields. Remotive descriptions are converted from HTML to plain
            text, removing scripts, styles, and other non-content elements while retaining readable
            text.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both sources use title keywords to infer seniority, with explicit precedence when
            several labels match. An unrecognized title becomes <code>unknown</code>. Remotive
            records are marked remote; Python.org’s remote flag comes from title and location
            wording, including checks for explicit negations.
          </p>
          <p>
            Malformed records are skipped. A failed request or an unrecognized source response fails
            the import. These are separate outcomes: incomplete data should not be mistaken for a
            successful fetch from a broken source.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <figure class="container system-trace" aria-describedby="system-caption">
        <figcaption>
          <span class="trace-label">SYSTEM TRACE</span>
          <span class="trace-title">From source data to ranked responses</span>
        </figcaption>
        <div class="trace-group">
          <p class="trace-label" id="import-label">
            IMPORT
          </p>
          <dl class="trace-stages" aria-labelledby="import-label">
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">01 / SOURCE</dt>
              <dd>Remotive JSON / Python.org listing HTML</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">02 / NORMALIZE</dt>
              <dd>Source-specific extraction and normalization → shared job record</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">03 / IDENTIFY</dt>
              <dd>Exact URL checks → per-record inserts</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">04 / STORE</dt>
              <dd>
                PostgreSQL<span class="trace-detail">unique URL constraint</span>
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-group">
          <p class="trace-label" id="read-label">
            READ
          </p>
          <dl class="trace-stages" aria-labelledby="read-label">
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">01 / REQUEST</dt>
              <dd>
                <code>GET /jobs</code>
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">02 / FILTER</dt>
              <dd>Database filters</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">03 / ORDER</dt>
              <dd>
                <dl class="trace-orders">
                  <div>
                    <dt>Date</dt>
                    <dd>SQL ordering → pagination</dd>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <dt>Score</dt>
                    <dd>
                      Load filtered candidates → calculate scores → sort in memory → select page
                    </dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">04 / RETURN</dt>
              <dd>
                Response with computed <code>match_score</code>
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="trace-stage">05 / CONSUME</dt>
              <dd>Frontend / API consumer</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <p class="system-caption" id="system-caption">
          HTTP import endpoints and the CLI share import services. Scores are calculated when
          returning records; score ordering also calculates them before pagination. Manual job
          creation writes directly to storage without importer normalization.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="duplicates-title">
        <p class="section-marker">02 / IDENTITY</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="duplicates-title">What counts as a duplicate</h2>
          <p>
            For the MVP, the practical priority was preventing repeated imports from creating
            duplicate offers.
          </p>
          <p>
            The shared persistence function checks incoming URLs against stored records and URLs
            already handled in the batch. A database unique constraint provides a second check. Each
            insert uses a savepoint, allowing a recognized URL conflict to be skipped while the
            remaining records are processed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Identity is based on the exact URL. Existing URLs are skipped without updating their
            records. Tracking parameters and URL aliases are not unified.
          </p>
          <p>
            This prevents repeated insertion of the same URL, but it does not identify every
            duplicate vacancy. The same role can appear on different websites with different
            URLs—and possibly different titles—and remain as separate records.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="api-title">
        <p class="section-marker">03 / API &amp; PERSISTENCE</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="api-title">From stored records to API responses</h2>
          <p>
            SQLAlchemy maps a single job model to PostgreSQL, with schema changes managed through
            Alembic. The stored timestamp records when an offer was added locally, not when the
            source published it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The API supports remote and seniority filters, text search, and limit/offset pagination.
            Text search checks title, company, location, and description; search wildcard characters
            are treated literally. Results can be ordered by date added or match score, with
            explicit tie-breaking rules.
          </p>
          <p>
            OpenAPI and Swagger expose the request and response structure. The frontend reads the
            same listing endpoint, keeping retrieval and ranking behavior in the backend.
          </p>
          <p>
            Imports run through protected HTTP endpoints or a one-shot CLI. The CLI attempts both
            sources with separate database sessions, so one source failure does not stop the other
            attempt.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="scoring-title">
        <p class="section-marker">04 / SCORING</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="scoring-title">A score for the roles I’m looking for</h2>
          <p>The score uses four inputs: title, description, remote status, and seniority.</p>
          <p>
            A relevance check runs first. A record needs a matching developer-role title or a
            Python, FastAPI, or SQLAlchemy signal before it receives points. Eligible records gain
            points for technology matches, a relevant title, remote work, and junior or intern
            seniority. Senior and leadership levels subtract points.
          </p>
          <p>
            Python contributes 20 points; FastAPI and PostgreSQL contribute 10 each. Each technology
            counts once, so repeated keywords cannot inflate the score. URLs and source metadata are
            removed from the scoring text.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result is calculated when records are returned, rather than stored. The same inputs
            under the same rules produce the same score, bounded between 0 and 100. It expresses
            keyword relevance to this profile, not hiring probability or semantic suitability.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ordering exposes a practical trade-off. Date ordering and pagination run in SQL. Score
            ordering loads all filtered candidates, calculates their scores in Python, sorts them,
            and then selects the requested page.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="verification-title">
        <p class="section-marker">05 / VERIFICATION</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="verification-title">What is verified</h2>
          <p>
            The pytest suite covers normalization, repeated imports, URL conflicts, scoring rules,
            filters, pagination, and ordering. It also checks malformed source responses, import
            authentication, and source-failure isolation in the CLI.
          </p>
          <p>
            External responses are mocked, and database tests use SQLite. PostgreSQL migration SQL
            is rendered and the driver is checked, but the suite does not exercise a live PostgreSQL
            server.
          </p>
          <p>
            GitHub Actions is configured to run pytest under Python 3.12 on pushes and pull requests
            targeting <code>main</code>. Docker Compose supplies a local application and PostgreSQL
            environment; database migrations remain an explicit step.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="limitations-title">
        <p class="section-marker">06 / LIMITS</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="limitations-title">Where the current approach stops</h2>
          <ul class="limitations">
            <li>
              <strong>Existing offers are not refreshed.</strong> Reimports leave stored content
              unchanged, and offers are not removed when they disappear from a source.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Source detail varies.</strong> Python.org listing summaries provide fewer
              scoring signals than full descriptions. Keyword-based seniority and remote inference
              can miss ambiguous wording.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Score sorting processes every filtered candidate.</strong> Its work grows with
              that result set, even when the requested page is small.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These are boundaries of the current implementation, not all deliberate strategies
            established at the beginning.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="container chapter" aria-labelledby="reflection-title">
        <p class="section-marker">07 / REFLECTION</p>
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h2 id="reflection-title">What I learned, and what I would change next</h2>
          <p>
            Scoring and duplicate handling were the most educational parts of the project. They
            required working with both the data available and the limits of what it could establish.
          </p>
          <p>
            I would add more job sources next: more useful offers, but also more varied data to test
            normalization, deduplication, and scoring assumptions. That variety could expose edge
            cases that do not appear with only two sources.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-16);
  }

  .introduction {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-12);
  }

  h1,
  h2 {
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  h2 {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-6);
    font-size: calc(var(--font-size-h3) + var(--space-1));
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }

  .chapter {
    max-inline-size: var(--content-width-reading);
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .chapter-copy {
    min-inline-size: 0;
    max-inline-size: var(--content-width-reading);
  }

  .chapter-copy > :last-child {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }

  .section-marker,
  .trace-label,
  .trace-stage {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  .section-marker {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-3);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: var(--font-size-body-large);
  }

  .back-link,
  .project-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-block-size: 3rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .project-link {
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }

  .system-trace {
    margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-8);
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
    max-inline-size: var(--content-width-reading);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-6);
  }

  figcaption .trace-label,
  .trace-title,
  .trace-detail {
    display: block;
  }

  figcaption .trace-label {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-2);
  }

  .trace-title {
    font-size: var(--font-size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--line-height-heading);
  }

  .trace-group + .trace-group {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }

  .trace-group > .trace-label {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-2);
  }

  .trace-stages,
  .trace-orders,
  dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .trace-stages > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .trace-stages dd {
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .trace-detail {
    font-size: var(--font-size-small);
  }

  .trace-orders > div + div {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  .trace-orders dt {
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }

  .system-caption {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-small);
  }

  .limitations {
    margin-block: 0 var(--space-6);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-6);
  }

  .limitations li::marker {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .limitations li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .trace-stages > div {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
      gap: var(--space-6);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .trace-orders > div {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: var(--space-12) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-3);
      align-items: baseline;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .case-study {
      padding-block-end: var(--space-24);
    }

    .introduction {
      margin-block-end: var(--space-16);
    }

    .chapter {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-8);
      max-inline-size: calc(var(--content-width-reading) / 0.75 + var(--space-8));
      padding-block: var(--space-12);
    }

    .section-marker {
      margin: 0;
      padding-block-start: var(--space-2);
    }
  }
</style>
